problem_statement_fixed:
The heap allocator offers named memory zones, each created with a reserved buffer of a fixed size (HEAPMEM_ZONE_DEFINE), and a caller allocates, frees, and reallocates memory through a zone via heapmem_zone_alloc(), heapmem_zone_free(), and heapmem_zone_realloc(), and queries a zone's usage with heapmem_zone_stats(). A caller expects each zone to manage its own reserved memory independently: an allocation in one zone must not consume capacity reserved for a different zone, an allocation larger than a zone's own reserved size must be rejected, freeing a pointer in a zone other than the one it was allocated from must be rejected, and a zone's usage statistics must reflect only that zone's state. The current implementation does not provide this: all zones draw from a single shared memory pool, so a large allocation in an otherwise-empty zone can fail when a different zone has fragmented its memory, allocations are not reliably bounded by a zone's own reserved size, and per-zone usage statistics do not reflect the state of the individual zone. The expected behavior is that each zone's reserved buffer is independent of the others, each allocation is bounded by the zone's own size, cross-zone frees fail, and a zone that has freed all its memory reports zero allocated memory and zero usage.

The public zone API must use a pointer to a zone, not an integer zone identifier. HEAPMEM_ZONE_DEFINE(name, bufsize) must define a zone object of type heapmem_zone_t and a static memory buffer of bufsize bytes for that zone; the zone is passed to the API by pointer. The zone functions must have the following observable signatures:
void *heapmem_zone_alloc(heapmem_zone_t *zone, size_t size);
bool heapmem_zone_free(heapmem_zone_t *zone, void *ptr);
void *heapmem_zone_realloc(heapmem_zone_t *zone, void *ptr, size_t size);
void heapmem_zone_stats(heapmem_zone_t *zone, heapmem_stats_t *stats)

heapmem_zone_alloc must fail with NULL for a request larger than the zone's reserved buffer size. heapmem_zone_free must return true only when ptr was allocated from that same zone and is currently allocated, and false otherwise; in particular, a pointer from a different zone must not be freed through another zone. heapmem_zone_realloc must behave like heapmem_zone_alloc when ptr is NULL, must free ptr and return NULL when size is zero, and otherwise must return a pointer to an allocation of the requested size with the old contents preserved.

When HEAPMEM_CONF_ARENA_SIZE is set, a general zone must be available and the existing general-zone convenience macros heapmem_alloc, heapmem_free, heapmem_realloc, heapmem_calloc, heapmem_stats, and heapmem_print_debug_info must operate on that general zone. A pointer allocated from the general zone must be freeable with heapmem_free, but not with heapmem_zone_free for a different zone. Zones and zone realloc must not require HEAPMEM_CONF_MAX_ZONES or HEAPMEM_CONF_REALLOC; those configuration options are not part of the zone interface.

heapmem_stats_t must expose the fields allocated, overhead, available, heap_usage, max_heap_usage, and chunks. heap_usage is the current zone heap usage, max_heap_usage is the high-water mark of heap_usage and is not reset when allocations are freed, and chunks is the number of currently allocated chunks. A zone that has freed all of its allocations must report allocated == 0. The minimum alignment of allocated objects must be exposed as HEAPMEM_ALIGNMENT in the public heapmem header, and this value must be a power of two.
